  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported travel surveys, which may underrepresent certain daily trips or informal mobility patterns.",
      "what": "This study analyzed travel surveys from five global cities to evaluate how daily mobility influences social mixing. The analysis revealed that individual activity spaces are the primary driver of variations in place exposure, exerting a stronger influence than demographic characteristics or proximity to transit.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that physical proximity to transit and demographic traits are insufficient proxies for actual social exposure, shifting the focus of urban mixing research toward the spatial boundaries of daily activities."
    }
  },
